.Dd December 21, 2026
.Dt ICOWM 5
.Os icowm-1.0.1
.Sh NAME
.Nm config.json
.Nd base configuration file for IcoWM
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Pa config.json
holds the base configuration of
.Xr icowm 1 :
how many screens and desktops there are, how windows are focused and
placed, what the icons and menus do, and what the system tray shows.
It lives in the configuration directory
.Xr icowm 1
resolves at startup.
.Pp
The file is JSON.
Every key is optional: one that is absent keeps its built-in default,
and so does every key of an absent object, so a file holding only
.Ql {}
is valid and changes nothing.
A key whose name begins with
.Ql -
or
.Ql _
is ignored outright, which is how a comment is written.
.Pp
Unless said otherwise below, a change takes effect on the next
configuration reload.
.Ss theme
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic theme Pq string, default Ql \&"default\&"
Name of the theme to load, without the
.Ql .json
suffix, from the
.Pa themes/
subdirectory.
When the named file is missing,
.Xr icowm 1
says so in the log and carries on with its built-in theme.
See
.Xr icowm-theme 5 .
.El
.Ss topology
Describes how many screens there are and how each divides into
desktops.
.Pp
This whole section is read only when the session starts.
Changing anything under it and reloading has no effect on a running
window manager: not the screen count, not how many desktops each screen
has, not a desktop's own name or background color.
Growing or shrinking the number of screens or desktops while running
would mean deciding what becomes of the clients, the focus and the EWMH
state already living on a desktop about to disappear, and reload does
none of that.
Restart to pick up a change here.
.Pp
Note that the sibling
.Ic desktops
section is a different thing despite the similar name: it describes
navigation and reserved space rather than topology, and does take effect
on reload like everything else.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic topology.screens.count Pq integer, default Ql 1
How many X screens to manage.
.It Ic topology.screens.desktops[].count Pq integer, default Ql 4
How many desktops that screen has, at most 16.
.It Ic topology.screens.desktops[].inaugural Pq integer, default Ql 0
Which desktop is current when the session starts.
.It Ic topology.screens.desktops[].settings[].name Pq string
Name of one desktop, shown in menus and in the switch overlay.
Defaults to
.Ql Desktop Ar N .
.It Ic topology.screens.desktops[].settings[].background-color Pq string
Background color of one desktop, as
.Ql #RRGGBB .
Absent, the theme's own desktop color is used.
.El
.Pp
A screen's desktops may be arranged as a grid rather than a flat list,
which is what lets navigation, the search box and the switch overlay
address a desktop by row and column and move north, south, east or
west:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic layout.orientation Pq string, default Ql \&"horizontal\&"
Whether consecutive desktops advance along a row
.Pq Ql horizontal
or down a column
.Pq Ql vertical .
.It Ic layout.corner Pq string, default Ql \&"top-left\&"
Which corner desktop 0 occupies:
.Ql top-left ,
.Ql top-right ,
.Ql bottom-left
or
.Ql bottom-right .
.It Ic layout.rows Pq integer, default Ql 1
.It Ic layout.columns Pq integer, default Ql 1
Size of the grid.
A layout with a single row or column behaves exactly as a flat list.
.El
.Ss programs
External commands the key bindings and menus launch.
Each is run through the shell, so arguments may be given.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic programs.terminal Pq string, default Ql \&"xterm\&"
.It Ic programs.launcher Pq string, default Ql \&"gmrun\&"
.It Ic programs.file-manager Pq string, default Ql \&"pcmanfm\&"
.It Ic programs.editor Pq string, default Ql \&"gvim\&"
.It Ic programs.web-browser Pq string, default Ql \&"firefox\&"
.El
.Ss windows
How ordinary windows behave.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic windows.gravity Pq string, default Ql \&"north-west\&"
Which corner or edge a window is anchored by when it has no gravity
hint of its own.
.It Ic windows.edges.snap.window Pq integer, default Ql 6
.It Ic windows.edges.snap.screen Pq integer, default Ql 6
How near, in pixels, a dragged window must come to another window's
border or to a screen edge before it snaps to it.
Zero disables snapping on that kind of edge.
.It Ic windows.edges.resistance Pq integer, default Ql 20
How far, in pixels, a drag must continue past an edge before the window
breaks free of it.
.It Ic windows.move-step Pq integer, default Ql 10
How many pixels each keyboard move shifts the focused window.
A value below 1 is treated as 1.
.It Ic windows.resize-step Pq integer, default Ql 20
How many pixels one step of a resize changes the axis being resized.
A value below 1 is treated as 1.
.Pp
This applies only to a window that publishes no usable size hints.
One that does, a terminal emulator sizing itself in whole character
cells being the usual case, is resized by whole increments of its own
instead, snapped from its base or minimum size, so it never lands on a
size it cannot render.
.Pp
That is true of the resize key bindings and of a mouse drag on a
border alike.
The one exception is the modal resize the window menu's
own
.Ql Resize
entry begins when that menu was opened from the keyboard: there the
step always applies, hints or not.
.It Ic windows.show-geom Pq boolean, default Ql true
Whether an overlay reports the position while a window is being moved
and the size while it is being resized.
.It Ic windows.solid-drag Pq boolean, default Ql true
Whether a window is moved live during a drag, or only an outline is,
the window itself jumping to its new place on release.
.It Ic windows.focus.policy Pq string, default Ql \&"click\&"
.Ql click
gives focus on a button press,
.Ql sloppy
gives it to whatever the pointer is over.
.It Ic windows.focus.focus-new Pq boolean, default Ql true
Whether a newly mapped window takes focus.
.It Ic windows.focus.raise Pq boolean, default Ql false
Whether a window is also raised when it gains focus by pointer or
wheel.
.Pp
This only ever adds raising, never takes it away.
Most ways of focusing a window raise it regardless, raising being what
was
asked for: clicking it, choosing it from the window list or the search
box, cycling to it, activating it from another application, and a
per-window rule that focuses it.
.Pp
What it governs is the few ways a window gains focus without anyone
asking it to come forward: the pointer entering it under
.Ql sloppy
focus, and the wheel over the desktop shading or unshading it.
It therefore makes no observable difference under
.Ql click
focus.
.It Ic windows.placement.policy Pq string, default Ql \&"smart\&"
Where a newly mapped window is put:
.Ql smart
looks for the position overlapping least of what is already on screen,
.Ql cascade
steps each new window down and to the right of the last,
.Ql centered
centers it on the workarea, and
.Ql under-mouse
puts it where the pointer is, and
.Ql manual
is reserved for asking where the window goes, which behaves as
.Ql smart
until that is written.
A transient or dialog window is centered on its parent regardless.
.It Ic windows.placement.monitor Pq string, default Ql \&"pointer\&"
Which monitor a new window lands on:
.Ql pointer ,
.Ql active ,
.Ql primary ,
or an explicit index.
.It Ic windows.placement.group-related Pq boolean, default Ql true
Whether a window is placed near others of its own application rather
than judged on its own.
.El
.Ss icons
How an iconified window is represented on the desktop.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic icons.show-geom Pq boolean, default Ql false
Whether an overlay reports the position while an icon is dragged.
.It Ic icons.placement.policy Pq string, default Ql \&"smart\&"
Where a new icon is put, the same policies windows have.
.El
.Ss shutdown and fortune
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic shutdown.enable-emergency-shortcut Pq boolean, default Ql false
Whether
.Ql Ctrl+Mod1+Backspace
terminates the session at once, with no dialog and no wait.
See
.Xr icowm 1 .
.It Ic shutdown.timeout-seconds Pq integer, default Ql 15
How long the ordinary quit action waits for applications to close
themselves before exiting anyway.
.It Ic fortune.is-enabled Pq boolean, default Ql true
Whether the fortune binding is grabbed at all.
.It Ic fortune.command Pq string, default Ql \&"fortune\&"
Command whose output that binding displays.
.El
.Ss startup-notification
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic startup-notification.timeout-seconds Pq integer, default Ql 15
How long a busy cursor is shown after launching an application before
giving up on it announcing itself.
.El
.Ss menus
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic menus.root.position Pq string, default Ql \&"under-mouse\&"
.It Ic menus.windows.position Pq string, default Ql \&"under-mouse\&"
Where the root menu and the window list open:
.Ql under-mouse ,
or a fixed corner of the workarea.
The root menu's contents come from a file of their own; see
.Xr icowm-menu 5 .
.El
.Ss systray
The docking area for XEmbed tray icons, and the clock and battery
readouts beside them.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic systray.is-enabled Pq boolean, default Ql true
Whether the tray is claimed and shown at all.
.It Ic systray.reserve-space Pq boolean, default Ql false
Whether the tray reserves a strut, so that maximized windows stop short
of it rather than passing underneath.
.It Ic systray.avoid-overlap Pq boolean, default Ql true
Whether window placement treats the tray as occupied even when it
reserves no space.
.It Ic systray.position Pq string, default Ql \&"top-left\&"
Which corner or edge of its monitor the tray docks against.
.It Ic systray.margins Pq object
Gap, in pixels, between the tray and each edge, through
.Ic top ,
.Ic right ,
.Ic bottom
and
.Ic left .
.It Ic systray.monitor Pq object
Which monitor holds the tray, through
.Ic anchor
and
.Ic index .
.It Ic systray.order Pq string, default Ql \&"left-to-right\&"
The direction icons accumulate in.
.It Ic systray.layer Pq string, default Ql \&"below\&"
Where the tray sits in the stacking order.
.It Ic systray.clock.is-enabled Pq boolean, default Ql true
.It Ic systray.clock.format Pq string, default Ql \&"%a %R\&"
Whether a clock is shown beside the icons, and in what format.
The format is handed to
.Xr strftime 3
unchanged, so any conversion it accepts may be used.
.It Ic systray.battery.is-enabled Pq boolean, default Ql false
Whether a battery readout is shown.
.It Ic systray.battery.threshold.charged Pq integer, default Ql 100
.It Ic systray.battery.threshold.low Pq integer, default Ql 20
.It Ic systray.battery.threshold.critical Pq integer, default Ql 5
Percentages at which the readout changes how it presents itself.
.It Ic systray.battery.backend.type Pq string, default Ql \&"acpi\&"
Where the charge is read from.
.It Ic systray.battery.backend.number Pq integer, default Ql 0
Which battery, on a machine with more than one.
.It Ic systray.battery.poll-seconds Pq integer, default Ql 30
How often it is read.
.It Ic systray.text.position Pq string, default Ql \&"left\&"
Which side of the icons the clock and battery text sits on.
Its spacing and vertical alignment are theme concerns instead; see
.Xr icowm-theme 5 .
.El
.Ss desktops
How switching between desktops behaves, and how much of each is left
free.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic desktops.show-overlay Pq boolean, default Ql true
Whether switching desktops announces which one became current.
.It Ic desktops.notify-activity Pq boolean, default Ql true
Whether a window demanding attention on another desktop is reported.
.It Ic desktops.warp-on-edge-drag Pq boolean, default Ql true
Whether dragging a window against a screen edge carries it through to
the neighboring desktop.
.It Ic desktops.wrap-at-bounds Pq boolean, default Ql true
Whether moving past the last desktop comes back around to the first.
.It Ic desktops.margins.top Pq integer, default Ql 0
.It Ic desktops.margins.right Pq integer, default Ql 0
.It Ic desktops.margins.bottom Pq integer, default Ql 0
.It Ic desktops.margins.left Pq integer, default Ql 0
Space, in pixels, kept clear at each edge of every desktop, on top of
whatever panels already reserve.
.El
.Ss scratchpad
A single window kept aside, shown and hidden by its own binding rather
than living on any desktop.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic scratchpad.is-enabled Pq boolean, default Ql true
Whether the scratchpad binding does anything.
.It Ic scratchpad.command Pq string
Command run to produce the scratchpad window on first use.
.It Ic scratchpad.edge Pq string, default Ql \&"top\&"
Which edge the window slides in from.
.It Ic scratchpad.ignore-margins Pq boolean, default Ql false
Whether it may cover the space
.Ic desktops.margins
keeps clear.
.El
.Ss prompt
A single always-centered field for typing a command directly, with no
application listing, no fuzzy matching and no cache, unlike
.Ic programs.launcher .
A mistyped or missing command shows an informational dialog rather than
failing silently, and a successful one closes the box at once.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic prompt.is-enabled Pq boolean
When true, the launcher binding opens this built-in box rather than
spawning
.Ic programs.launcher .
Defaults to false ordinarily, and to true under restricted-memory mode,
which prefers it precisely to avoid the extra process.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME}/icowm/config.json
The file this page describes, at the first of the several locations
.Xr icowm 1
looks in.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
A file that changes only what it means to change, everything else
keeping its default:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{
    "theme": "default",
    "windows": {
        "focus": { "policy": "sloppy" },
        "placement": { "policy": "cascade" }
    },
    "topology": {
        "screens": [
            { "desktops": { "count": 6, "inaugural": 0 } }
        ]
    }
}
.Ed
.Pp
Two rows of three desktops, navigated by direction, with the first at
the top left:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{
    "topology": {
        "screens": [
            {
                "desktops": {
                    "count": 6,
                    "layout": {
                        "orientation": "horizontal",
                        "corner": "top-left",
                        "rows": 2,
                        "columns": 3
                    }
                }
            }
        ]
    }
}
.Ed
.Pp
Check a file for syntax errors without starting a session:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
icowm -C -c ~/.config/icowm
.Ed
